title: nemesis1_ambush
---
Narrator:
The ambush is not designed to destroy you.

---

Narrator:
That is the first thing your tactical officer notes. If they wanted you dead, you would not have had time to reach this conclusion.

The positioning is wrong for a kill. It is right for an assessment.

---

Narrator:
Three vessels. Unknown class. They hold at extreme range and paint your ships with targeting sensors for approximately two minutes.

Then they withdraw on a heading that your navigator cannot track.

---

Narrator:
Your tactical officer says: they ran a targeting drill. They wanted to know how we respond under threat.

Your security officer says: they already knew. They just wanted to confirm it.

---

-> [Pursue — force a confrontation]
    <<apply_effect "nemesis_intel">>
    Narrator:
    You push the fleet to intercept speed. Your navigator plots four probable withdrawal vectors.

    Narrator:
    You're right about one of them.

    Narrator:
    The resulting engagement is brief and inconclusive. The nemesis vessels pull back without losing a ship.

    Narrator:
    But you learned their drive signatures. Your tactical officer considers this a significant win.

-> [Hold position — let them go]
    <<apply_effect "nemesis_intel">>
    <<chain_event "nemesis1_messenger">>
    Narrator:
    You hold. The fleet goes to battle stations but does not pursue.

    Narrator:
    Ten minutes later, one of the three vessels returns alone. It hails you on a standard channel.

    Narrator:
    Your security officer says: this is what they were waiting for.

===
